;; og:preserve-this wvehicle-weapons-vein rung 6: process-nettable-method-15
;; on mine-a ("net message pack"), mine-b's own 15/17 and v-yellow-guided-
;; missile-method-80 all decode clean off this object's own scoped decode;
;; landed together as the rung's own mass-mining item. mine-a's own op-41
;; wall (l.hu (+ v1 158)) was a missing narrowing cast: the type? guard
;; just above it (ops 32-36) proves v1 is process-nettable but does not
;; narrow it for the decompiler, so the read still saw the pre-check plain
;; process; type_casts.jsonc's own new "(method 15 mine-a)" entry casts v1
;; to process-nettable for ops 37-41 (the only path that reaches the read
;; without hitting the op-38 #f short-circuit first), resolving machine
;; offset 158 = declared 162 (the +4 basic-tag trap) as process-nettable's
;; own net-id field -- exactly the method's own declared purpose. mine-b's
;; own 15/17 walls were the same class: write-handle and read-handle
;; (all-types.gc) were both already "activated" as live externs but left
;; typed bare "function", which fails at the CALL site itself (not a state
;; slot) with the same "we do not know its type" the init-by-other class
;; hits; both retyped from their own two call sites' evidence (a mem-
;; buffer-as-symbol receiver every sibling mem-buffer pack call in this
;; file uses, and a uint read off mine-b's own shooter/glued-to handle
;; fields for write-handle, machine offset 252/260 = declared 256/264;
;; uint not uint64 despite the fields' own uint64 declaration -- goalc's
;; function-argument typecheck rejected a uint64 the-as here with "got a
;; uint", matching this file's own already-landed mine-b-method-65's own
;; (the-as uint #f) convention on the same fields).
;;
;; Two verbatim orphan-tell markers, NOT reconstructed into their likely
;; intended call shape (the jakx-phase1-landing-pipeline orphan-tell
;; corollary, issue 531: a landed caller carrying the tell IS the defect,
;; not something a landing rung invents a fix for): mine-a-method-15's own
;; "(-> v1-10 net-id) (t9-4)" pair (mem-buffer-method-19, called bare
;; immediately after an orphaned read of the value it almost certainly
;; consumes) and mine-b-method-17's own "enter-state (-> this glued-to)
;; (-> this attach-point) (go ...)" sequence (go compiles to (go-hook pp
;; next-state args...), gstate.gc, so the two orphaned reads are very
;; likely go-hook's own intended extra args) are both transcribed exactly
;; as the decompiler printed them and both compile as printed.
;;
;; One build-time deviation, the same class documented elsewhere in this
;; file: mine-a-method-15's own raw pid<<32|pointer triple (nonzero?/
;; l32-false-check/sllv-r0/pid-compare over this's own shooter handle) is
;; handle->process's own compiled expansion (gkernel-h.gc); substituted the
;; real macro call.
;;
;; v-yellow-guided-missile-method-80 needed no config changes at all
;; (decoded clean once mine-b's own write-handle/read-handle fix landed,
;; which this object's own top-level construction touches before method
;; 80's own code); its own trailing bare 0 before (none) matches this
;; file's own gunmount-drawable-method-51 precedent (wvehicle-weapons.gc),
;; kept verbatim.
;;
;; mine-b-method-35 tried and reverted: its own wall (op 49, offset 28,
;; "Could not figure out load: (set! v1 (l.wu (+ a0 28)))") clears with the
;; same mem-buffer-as-int-arg0 pattern every sibling call in this function
;; uses, decoding a full clean body that calls mem-buffer-method-45 through
;; method-of-type and pattern-matches its return against 'idle/'shake-off/
;; 'falling/'attached/'leaping-onto. mem-buffer-method-45 is declared
;; () none (a bare zero-arg placeholder, not even a _type_ receiver) and
;; goalc will not compile a call against it at all -- "Expected 0 arguments
;; but got 1" with the receiver supplied, "get_preferred_reg_class called
;; on NullType" (a compiler crash, not a clean rejection) with the bare
;; form as printed, since the body's own let binds its none-typed result
;; to a variable and pattern-matches it against symbols. mem-buffer-h.gc's
;; own og:preserve-this note on method 45 (methods 18/19/20/21/28/45)
;; already investigated this exact slot and held it deliberately: its raw
;; body reads an uninitialized a0 and contains an inline instruction the
;; decompiler cannot render at all (the raw address-into-symbol-table
;; idiom), and net-enemy.gc's own call site has the identical bare zero-
;; arg shape with nothing to supply either register -- "a systemic decode
;; gap in this whole zero-arg-declared method family... held rather than
;; guessed." Retyping the signature to unblock this one caller without
;; being able to land method-45's own body would silently paper over that
;; documented gap on a type used across dozens of objects in the corpus,
;; a much wider blast radius than this rung's own scope; left held, not
;; landed, matching that prior call.
;;
;; Held, not landed this rung, fresh walls documented in the relevant
;; config files (type_casts.jsonc / hacks.jsonc's own notes): mine-a-
;; method-35 (op 49, offset 28 off net-process-mgr-method-46's own return,
;; declared none but evidently used -- the same defect class as write-
;; handle/read-handle above, but on a shared corpus type with a much wider
;; blast radius to retype), and v-yellow-guided-missile's own methods 67
;; (op 1), 70 (op 2) and 79 (op 52), each its own independent offset-28/
;; offset-284/offset-136 wall this rung found no in-band evidence to
;; resolve safely.
;;
(define-extern write-handle (function mem-buffer uint none))
(define-extern read-handle (function mem-buffer uint))

;; definition for method 15 of type mine-a
(defmethod process-nettable-method-15 ((this mine-a) (arg0 symbol) (arg1 mem-buffer))
  ((method-of-type mem-buffer mem-buffer-method-23) (the-as mem-buffer arg0) (-> this root trans))
  ((method-of-type mem-buffer mem-buffer-method-23) (the-as mem-buffer arg0) (-> this destination-point))
  ((method-of-type mem-buffer mem-buffer-method-25) (the-as mem-buffer arg0) (-> this destination-rot))
  (let ((v1-10 (as-type (handle->process (-> this shooter)) process-nettable)))
    (cond
      (v1-10
        (let ((t9-4 (method-of-type mem-buffer mem-buffer-method-19)))
          (-> v1-10 net-id)
          (t9-4)
          )
        )
      (else
        (let ((t9-5 (method-of-type mem-buffer mem-buffer-method-19)))
          #xffff
          (t9-5)
          )
        )
      )
    )
  (none)
  )

